WEBVTT

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:02.561
Trace the proof back to its two inputs.

00:00:02.561 --> 00:00:06.416
Read the numbers here as the lemma numbers in Bandtlow’s preprint.

00:00:06.416 --> 00:00:11.657
Lesson four, Lemma one point seven, moves the real coefficient onto an ellipse.

00:00:11.657 --> 00:00:17.624
Lesson five, Lemma one point eight, tells us which kernel waves survive on its preimage circle.

00:00:17.624 --> 00:00:21.030
These are the two direct inputs to Lemma one point nine.

00:00:21.030 --> 00:00:22.795
Look one step further back.

00:00:22.795 --> 00:00:31.535
In the proof of the kernel lemma, the positive Laurent weights from Lesson one, Lemma one point one, give the surviving coefficients their sign.

00:00:31.535 --> 00:00:37.502
Real line stability from Lesson two, Lemma one point two, bounds each coefficient by two.

00:00:37.502 --> 00:00:39.117
Keep Lesson three in sight.

00:00:39.117 --> 00:00:44.423
We will use its ellipse growth bound when we put the polynomial series back together.

00:00:44.423 --> 00:00:46.276
Read the ellipse from its circle.

00:00:46.276 --> 00:00:54.063
Start with the cyan point z on the smaller circle of radius s, strictly between one and the fixed outer radius rho.

00:00:54.063 --> 00:00:55.465
Its angle is theta.

00:00:55.465 --> 00:01:02.677
The Joukowski map takes it to the gold point omega on the boundary of the inner integration ellipse, E sub s.

00:01:02.677 --> 00:01:04.804
Move both points with the same angle.

00:01:04.804 --> 00:01:09.128
The function lives on the ellipse, so we read its value at the gold point.

00:01:09.128 --> 00:01:15.244
Writing that very same value as f of J of z makes it a function of the circle point.

00:01:15.244 --> 00:01:17.122
That composition is the pullback.

00:01:17.122 --> 00:01:19.868
For the contour integral, carry the tangent too.

00:01:19.868 --> 00:01:24.341
The step on the ellipse is J prime of z times the step on the circle.

00:01:24.341 --> 00:01:29.754
Composing the kernel with this same map lets us read its waves in the circle angle.

00:01:29.754 --> 00:01:31.777
See radius act on one wave.

00:01:31.777 --> 00:01:38.562
The pullback of the second kind kernel has only negative powers of z, by the expansion in Lesson five.

00:01:38.562 --> 00:01:44.259
To see what a single power does, substitute s times the exponential of i theta for z.

00:01:44.259 --> 00:01:52.025
Raising it to the power minus k gives a length of one over s to the k, and an angle of minus k times theta.

00:01:52.025 --> 00:01:54.820
Turn the point around the circle at fixed radius.

00:01:54.820 --> 00:01:57.530
The directions change and the lengths stay fixed.

00:01:57.530 --> 00:02:02.374
Now freeze theta and increase the circle radius from one point two four to two.

00:02:02.374 --> 00:02:06.620
All three arrows keep their directions, but higher powers shorten faster.

00:02:06.620 --> 00:02:10.304
At radius two, the fourth power has length one sixteenth.

00:02:10.304 --> 00:02:12.537
The sixth has length one sixty-fourth.

00:02:12.537 --> 00:02:15.581
The eighth has length one two-hundred-and-fifty-sixth.

00:02:15.581 --> 00:02:20.076
These are the lengths before multiplying by each kernel coefficient.

00:02:20.076 --> 00:02:23.344
Follow the first kernel wave as degree increases.

00:02:23.344 --> 00:02:28.529
Each coloured row now represents a different Legendre degree on the same inner contour.

00:02:28.529 --> 00:02:35.634
In any one row the kernel frequencies advance by two, so the length bounds decrease by a factor of one over s squared.

00:02:35.634 --> 00:02:37.640
Look down the first column instead.

00:02:37.640 --> 00:02:44.105
For degrees two, three, and four, the first allowed powers are minus three, minus four, and minus five.

00:02:44.105 --> 00:02:51.188
Raising the degree by one therefore moves the whole geometric majorant down by a factor of one over s.

00:02:51.188 --> 00:02:56.693
The pullback exposes the negative powers, and the kernel lemma tells us which powers can occur.

00:02:56.693 --> 00:03:02.325
Summing a row and using the contour estimate gives a bound containing s to the minus n.

00:03:02.325 --> 00:03:10.447
Let the inner contour radius approach the fixed outer radius rho to obtain the coefficient bound with rho to the minus n.

00:03:10.447 --> 00:03:12.791
Take the root to read the decay rate.

00:03:12.791 --> 00:03:21.873
For a fixed outer ellipse, Lemma one point nine bounds the coefficient by a constant, times two n plus one, times rho to the minus n.

00:03:21.873 --> 00:03:24.576
Take the n-th root for positive n.

00:03:24.576 --> 00:03:26.768
The root of the prefactor tends to one.

00:03:26.768 --> 00:03:31.440
The remaining factor is exactly one over rho, the mint horizontal line.

00:03:31.440 --> 00:03:35.174
Follow the violet upper envelope of the roots toward that line.

00:03:35.174 --> 00:03:40.203
The upper limit of the actual coefficient roots is therefore at most one over rho.

00:03:40.203 --> 00:03:46.326
For our fixed function, the cyan dots give actual coefficient roots at the displayed integer degrees.

00:03:46.326 --> 00:03:49.542
The ellipse supplies a ceiling on their limiting rate.

00:03:49.542 --> 00:03:51.651
Rebuild the function on the interval.

00:03:51.651 --> 00:03:54.844
A small coefficient is one part of a small term.

00:03:54.844 --> 00:03:58.294
We also need the size of the polynomial it multiplies.

00:03:58.294 --> 00:04:04.837
On the red interval, Lesson two, Lemma one point two, bounds every Legendre polynomial by one.

00:04:04.837 --> 00:04:09.353
The omitted terms are thus bounded by the sum of the coefficient bounds.

00:04:09.353 --> 00:04:11.587
Increase the number of retained terms.

00:04:11.587 --> 00:04:17.937
Watch the partial polynomial approach the fixed function, and watch its error on a separate logarithmic scale.

00:04:17.937 --> 00:04:21.174
The geometric tail tends to zero uniformly.

00:04:21.174 --> 00:04:27.035
Completeness of the Legendre basis identifies the limit with our function on the interval.

00:04:27.035 --> 00:04:29.143
Which ellipse supplies each power?

00:04:29.143 --> 00:04:34.044
Choose the violet outer ellipse on which the function is bounded and holomorphic.

00:04:34.044 --> 00:04:38.880
Its parameter rho is the radius of the circle before the Joukowski map.

00:04:38.880 --> 00:04:45.430
Choose a smaller gold target ellipse where we evaluate the reconstructed function, with circle radius r.

00:04:45.430 --> 00:04:48.460
The marks on the red interval are their common foci.

00:04:48.460 --> 00:04:50.502
Follow the gold factor first.

00:04:50.502 --> 00:05:00.786
Lesson three, Bandtlow’s Lemma one point six, part a, bounds the size of each degree n basis polynomial throughout the gold target ellipse by r to the n.

00:05:00.786 --> 00:05:02.848
Now follow the violet factor.

00:05:02.848 --> 00:05:09.633
Lesson six, Lemma one point nine, contributes rho to the minus n from the outer coefficient estimate.

00:05:09.633 --> 00:05:13.687
Its prefactor includes the fixed outer norm and perimeter constant.

00:05:13.687 --> 00:05:21.944
Multiply the gold mode growth by the violet coefficient decay to obtain the mint ratio, r over rho to the power n.

00:05:21.944 --> 00:05:28.323
For fixed nested ellipses this ratio is below one and the resulting upper bounds have a summable tail.

00:05:28.323 --> 00:05:33.103
The uniform reconstruction estimate is a consequence of these two lemmas together.

00:05:33.103 --> 00:05:37.001
Lemma one point nine itself bounds a single coefficient.

00:05:37.001 --> 00:05:39.601
Move the gold ellipse, then hold it fixed.

00:05:39.601 --> 00:05:44.031
Keep the outer radius at two point one five and keep the same function.

00:05:44.031 --> 00:05:46.948
Begin with a gold radius of one point two nine.

00:05:46.948 --> 00:05:48.627
The ratio is six tenths.

00:05:48.627 --> 00:05:51.394
Move the gold rim closer to the violet rim.

00:05:51.394 --> 00:05:55.845
At a radius of one point nine three five, the ratio is nine tenths.

00:05:55.845 --> 00:05:59.337
The coefficients stay the same because the function is fixed.

00:05:59.337 --> 00:06:02.638
Each basis mode now has a larger allowed size.

00:06:02.638 --> 00:06:07.644
To guarantee the same accuracy on the larger ellipse, our bound needs more terms.

00:06:07.644 --> 00:06:14.507
At the outer rim the ratio reaches one and this geometric estimate gives no decay guarantee.

00:06:14.507 --> 00:06:18.003
Keep the gold target and enlarge the violet ellipse.

00:06:18.003 --> 00:06:20.813
Hold the target radius at one point two nine.

00:06:20.813 --> 00:06:23.622
Start the outer radius at one point seven two.

00:06:23.622 --> 00:06:27.072
The target occupies three quarters of that circle radius.

00:06:27.072 --> 00:06:31.247
Now expand only the violet ellipse, up to two point one five.

00:06:31.247 --> 00:06:36.702
The ratio falls to six tenths, so the geometric factor decays faster with degree.

00:06:36.702 --> 00:06:40.429
The coefficients themselves are still those of the same function.

00:06:40.429 --> 00:06:48.849
Its supremum and the perimeter constant can change on a larger domain, so the full numerical bound also depends on its prefactor.

00:06:48.849 --> 00:06:54.880
We can use a larger violet ellipse only while the function remains bounded and analytic throughout it.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:06:59.525
In this example the pole at two and a half limits how far we can go.

00:06:59.525 --> 00:07:02.423
Fix the violet outer ellipse for both curves.

00:07:02.423 --> 00:07:05.553
The violet outer ellipse is E two point one five.

00:07:05.553 --> 00:07:14.058
It stays fixed for both curves and supplies the coefficient bound because the function is bounded and analytic throughout this outer ellipse.

00:07:14.058 --> 00:07:23.310
The cyan curve gives an upper bound on the error throughout the smaller inner ellipse, E one point two nine, where r divided by rho is six tenths.

00:07:23.310 --> 00:07:31.240
The pink curve gives the bound throughout the larger inner ellipse, E one point nine three five, where the ratio is nine tenths.

00:07:31.240 --> 00:07:40.854
Each bound holds throughout its target interior and, by continuity, on its boundary, since both closed targets lie inside the violet outer ellipse.

00:07:40.854 --> 00:07:45.818
Keep all three ellipse sizes fixed and increase only N, the number of terms kept.

00:07:45.818 --> 00:07:49.758
The dashed line marks the absolute tolerance of one hundredth.

00:07:49.758 --> 00:07:54.743
Both curves use the same elementary perimeter estimate on the outer ellipse.

00:07:54.743 --> 00:08:02.139
On E one point two nine, nineteen terms first guarantee this accuracy everywhere on that target using our bound.

00:08:02.139 --> 00:08:08.169
On E one point nine three five, the corresponding count is one hundred and twenty one terms.

00:08:08.169 --> 00:08:12.984
These are sufficient counts from the estimate, and the actual error can be smaller.

00:08:12.984 --> 00:08:21.126
On each fixed inner target, the upper bound for the whole tail tends to zero as N grows, because its ratio is strictly below one.

00:08:21.126 --> 00:08:27.946
The identity theorem identifies each limit with the function that the series recovers on the real interval.

00:08:27.946 --> 00:08:34.596
For this rational function the finite partial sums have positive uniform error on each inner target.

00:08:34.596 --> 00:08:44.489
At the violet outer radius itself, r would equal rho and the ratio would be one, so this estimate gives no uniform convergence guarantee there.

